1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. d3 {Sergeant was fond
of this quiet move, and sometimes played it a move earlier (the Anderssen
variation).} b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. Nbd2 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. Re1 Nc6 12. Nf1
Re8 13. Ne3 Bf8 14. h3 Bb7 15. Nf5 Ne7 16. g4 Ng6 17. a4 d5 18. axb5 axb5 19.
Bg5 h6 20. Rxa8 Bxa8 21. Bd2 c4 {A good example of hitting the base of a pawn
chain. White's weakness on the long diagonal becomes apparent.} 22. g5 cxd3 23.
Bxd3 dxe4 24. Bxb5 exf3 25. gxf6 Qxf6 26. Bxe8 Qxf5 27. Kh2 e4 28. Be3 (28.
Bxf7+ Kxf7 29. Qb3+ Bd5 30. Qb8 Be6 $19) 28... Qe5+ 29. Kh1 ({Corrected from} 
29. Kg1 {, 16 December 2023. Both sources cited give K-R1.}) 29... Bd6 {
Sources: BCM, February 1953, p39; Source: "Chess for the Love of It" by A. R.
B. Thomas (Routledge & Kegan Paul, 1973), p53} 0-1

